0 Stonehenge - Sarsen Circle (30 uprights + lintel ring) and the inner
0 Trilithon Horseshoe (5 trilithons), a directorial scale model for shot
0 A1-S06 "Stonehenge erhebt sich"
0 Name: stonehenge.ldr
0 Author: spex project (hand-authored placement data; not sourced from an
0 official ldraw.org models/ release)
0 //
0 // SOURCE: real-world dimensions below are drawn from Cleal, R.M.J.,
0 // Walker, K.E. & Montague, R., "Stonehenge in its Landscape: Twentieth
0 // Century Excavations" (English Heritage Archaeological Report 10,
0 // 1995), as summarised on English Heritage's own site pages
0 // (english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/stonehenge/history-and-stories/
0 // history/description/ and .../things-to-do/stone-circle/stones-of-
0 // stonehenge/) and cross-checked against the Wikipedia "Stonehenge"
0 // article's "Sarsen structures" section, which cites the same report.
0 // Numbers used:
0 //   Sarsen Circle: ~30m (98ft) diameter, 30 original uprights, each
0 //     ~4.11m (13.5ft) tall above ground, ~2.13m (7ft) wide, ~1.06m
0 //     (3.5ft) deep; average gap between uprights ~1m (3.3ft); lintels
0 //     ~3.2m (10ft) long x 1m (3.3ft) wide x 0.8m (2.6ft) thick, tops
0 //     ~4.9m (16ft) above ground.
0 //   Trilithon Horseshoe: 5 trilithons (10 uprights + 5 lintels), graded
0 //     in height and symmetric about the tallest: the smallest pair
0 //     ~6m (20ft), the next pair "a little higher" (English Heritage's
0 //     own wording - no published figure exists for this pair; this
0 //     file uses 6.65m, the arithmetic midpoint of the documented 6m
0 //     and 7.3m figures, flagged here as an interpolation, not a
0 //     citation), and the Great Trilithon ~7.3m (24ft) above ground -
0 //     one of its two uprights (Stone 55) has since fallen; the
0 //     standing upright (Stone 56) is documented as 6.7m visible + 2.4m
0 //     below ground = 9.1m, matching the ~9m/~10m pair length also
0 //     reported for the two Great Trilithon stones.
0 //
0 // SCALE: 1 real metre = 100 LDU in this file. True LDraw scale (1 LDU
0 // = 0.4mm) would put the 7.3m Great Trilithon at ~18,250 LDU next to
0 // the 184-LDU monolith (monolith.ldr) - a speck beside a skyscraper.
0 // At 100 LDU/m the Great Trilithon stands 720 LDU, about 3.9x the
0 // monolith's height: "a few times the monolith's height", per the
0 // brief, not a few hundred thousand. Every other dimension below is
0 // this one factor x its own real-world figure - one decision, not one
0 // guess per part.
0 //
0 // HEIGHTS (real x 100, rounded to the nearest buildable course - LDraw
0 // bricks/plates only stack in multiples of 8 LDU, so a target that
0 // isn't itself a multiple of 8 cannot be hit exactly, the same limit
0 // monolith.ldr's own header proves for its 1:4:9 ratio):
0 //   Circle upright:   411 LDU real -> 17 courses x 3x Brick 2x10
0 //     (3006.dat, 24 LDU tall per course, same as every other real
0 //     brick here - see REVISION below) = 408 LDU (off by 3 LDU / 1.2mm)
0 //   Circle lintel:    320 LDU real -> 4 x Brick 1x4 (3010.dat, 80 LDU
0 //     each) laid end to end = 320 LDU (exact)
0 //   Short trilithon:  600 LDU real -> 25 courses x 3x 3006.dat = 600
0 //     LDU (exact)
0 //   Mid trilithon:    665 LDU real (interpolated, see above) -> 28
0 //     courses x 3x 3006.dat = 672 LDU (off by 7 LDU / 2.8mm)
0 //   Great trilithon:  730 LDU real -> 30 courses x 3x 3006.dat = 720
0 //     LDU (off by 10 LDU / 4mm)
0 //   Trilithon lintel: 526 LDU (structural derivation: 2x the 213-LDU
0 //     upright width + the cited ~100-LDU average sarsen spacing, since
0 //     no trilithon-specific lintel length is published) -> 7 x
0 //     3010.dat = 560 LDU (off by 34 LDU / 13.6mm)
0 //
0 // STYLE (revised after review - see REVISION below): like monolith.ldr,
0 // this file matches real HEIGHT and real horizontal POSITION (circle
0 // diameter, upright count, upright angular spacing - all via real
0 // trigonometry off the cited 1500-LDU radius) and now ALSO approximates
0 // real stone WIDTH/DEPTH with real footprint geometry, using a wider
0 // real part rather than tiling the small ones: every upright course
0 // (circle and trilithon alike) is 3x Brick 2x10 (3006.dat, a real,
0 // standard LDraw part - 40x200 LDU footprint per brick) laid side by
0 // side, giving a built cross-section of 120x200 LDU against the cited
0 // 106x213 LDU (2.13m x 1.06m) real upright - within 13%/6%, not within
0 // an order of magnitude. This is the same "real footprint, real part"
0 // discipline BRICKs.md holds every other spex demo to; the earlier
0 // single-3004/3010-per-course version (20x40 LDU, ~10x too thin) was a
0 // real miscalibration, not a deliberate style choice - see REVISION.
0 // Lintels are unchanged: Brick 1x4 (3010.dat, the same part monolith.ldr
0 // is built from), one course per lintel row, since neither their real
0 // footprint nor their real thickness was the review's finding. Lintel
0 // THICKNESS remains stylized to one 24-LDU brick course rather than the
0 // real 80-LDU (0.8m) proportion - the same "swap, don't add" economy
0 // monolith.ldr uses for its own tile course.
0 //
0 // The Trilithon Horseshoe's own radius (900 LDU) and its 5 positions'
0 // angular spread (120/150/180/210/240 degrees) remain a placement/
0 // layout decision, NOT a cited dimension - no published figure for the
0 // horseshoe's own diameter was found in the sources above; 900 LDU is
0 // sized to read clearly inside the cited 1500-LDU circle radius. The
0 // offset between the two uprights of one trilithon pair is NOT a
0 // layout choice (a prior version of this file said it was, at 120 LDU
0 // - wrong, see REVISION): it is derived, the same way the lintel length
0 // above already was - 313 LDU, the 526-LDU lintel length minus one
0 // 213-LDU upright width, so the lintel's own two ends land flush with
0 // the outer face of each upright rather than overhanging past them.
0 //
0 // REVISION (2026-08-09, addressing github.com/tronicum/stardate PR #42
0 // review): the original file built every upright as a single small
0 // brick (3004.dat/3010.dat) stacked in place - correct in height and
0 // circle position, but roughly 10x too thin in the cited real width and
0 // 2.5x too thin in the cited real depth, which also meant every
0 // trilithon's lintel (sized correctly, per the citation above) badly
0 // overhung its own too-thin, too-close-together uprights and read as a
0 // capital T rather than a trilithon. Fixed by widening every upright
0 // (not the lintels) to 3x Brick 2x10 (3006.dat) per course and
0 // correcting the trilithon pair offset from the old, undocumented 120
0 // LDU to the derived 313 LDU above. Part count rose from 937 to 2501 -
0 // still comfortably inside "a few hundred to a few thousand", and far
0 // below the "tens of thousands" the file's own prior header wrongly
0 // assumed a true-footprint build would cost (that estimate re-tiled the
0 // small 3004.dat/3010.dat bricks across the full real footprint instead
0 // of switching to a wider real part - the real lesson here, not a
0 // one-off numeric miss). 3006.dat's exact real triangle geometry was
0 // NOT re-verified against a local render in this pass (no network
0 // access to fetch it into .ldraw-cache/ in this session) - its real
0 // part number and nominal footprint (40x200 LDU = 2x10 studs) are a
0 // standard, extremely well-established LDraw/BrickLink part, the same
0 // level of confidence 3004/3005/3010's numbers already had before this
0 // repository's own spex-ldraw fetch verified them; flagging the gap
0 // rather than asserting it's closed. Whoever next builds/renders this
0 // scene with network access should run it through spex mesh-model once
0 // to confirm.
0 //
0 // ORIENTATION: LDraw's own +Y-DOWN convention applies throughout -
0 // translations grow more NEGATIVE as height increases, exactly as
0 // monolith.ldr's header warns (it was gotten wrong once before here).
0 // Ground level is y=0; every course below sits at y = -(course_count
0 // x 24), each part's bottom landing exactly on the real top of
0 // whatever is stacked below it, the same convention monolith.ldr uses.
0 //
0 // ROTATION: each upright and lintel carries a standard Y-axis rotation
0 // matrix turning it to face the circle's/horseshoe's centre at its own
0 // angular position theta: a=cos(theta) c=sin(theta) g=-sin(theta)
0 // i=cos(theta), b=d=f=h=0, e=1. Identity (theta=0) is
0 // "1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1", the same no-rotation convention monolith.ldr
0 // itself uses.
0 //
0 // COLOUR (revised, see REVISION above): 72 (Dark Bluish Grey, #646464
0 // per LDConfig.ldr) - corrected from the prior version's 71 (Light
0 // Bluish Gray, #969696), which was picked to match the screenplay's own
0 // "Steingrau" (linear [0.14996, 0.155926, 0.138432] = sRGB #6C6E68) on
0 // the belief that a material track could rebind a scene's declared
0 // colour at runtime. It cannot: this format's material track (M62/M65)
0 // only carries opacity/roughness/metalness/emissiveIntensity/
0 // transmission/edgeOpacity - never colour. The colour actually declared
0 // on a placement line IS what renders, so it has to be the closest real
0 // LDraw code to Steingrau, which is 72, not 71.
0 //
0 // BUILD ORDER / STEPs: the shot is "Stonehenge erhebt sich" - Stonehenge
0 // rises. STEPs go: all 30 circle uprights (one per STEP), then the 30
0 // lintels that cap the ring, then the 5 trilithon upright pairs
0 // (shortest to tallest: short, short, mid, mid, Great), then their 5
0 // lintels in the same order - so a later runtime choreographer (M64,
0 // not this file's job) can raise the whole monument stone by stone,
0 // climaxing on the Great Trilithon.
0 //
0 // PARTS (revised, see REVISION above): (30 circle uprights x 17
0 // courses + (2 short x 25 + 2 mid x 28 + 1 great x 30) x 2 trilithon
0 // uprights) x 3 (the 3006.dat widening) + 30 circle lintels x 4 + 5
0 // trilithon lintels x 7 (lintels unwidened) = (510 + 272) x 3 + 120 +
0 // 35 = 2346 + 155 = 2501 parts, comfortably inside the brief's "few
0 // hundred to a few thousand" - up from the prior 937, still nowhere
0 // near the "tens of thousands" a full small-brick re-tile would have
0 // cost (see REVISION).
0 //
0 // Instance ids are generated by show-build as stonehenge/<n> from this
0 // scene's "prefix": "stonehenge" - nothing to declare in this file for
0 // that; the existing "stonehenge/**" target in the show document
0 // matches unchanged.
0 --- SARSEN CIRCLE: 30 uprights, evenly spaced, radius 1500 LDU ---
